import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,a3 as o}from"./chunks/framework.D1GAkLkK.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Compatibility","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference/compatibility.md","filePath":"reference/compatibility.md"}'),r={name:"reference/compatibility.md"};function d(i,e,n,c,h,l){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="compatibility" tabindex="-1">Compatibility <a class="header-anchor" href="#compatibility" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Compatibility&quot;">​</a></h1><p>OpsTruss follows <a href="https://semver.org" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Semantic Versioning</a>. Within a major version, such as 1.x, a minor or patch release keeps every project, component, plugin, and script that reads <code>--json</code> working as it did, without a change on their side.</p><h2 id="what-stays" tabindex="-1">What stays <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-stays" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What stays&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Promised</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Files</td><td>The keys of <code>opstruss.yaml</code>, <code>opstruss.plugin.yaml</code>, <code>opstruss.catalog.yaml</code>, and <code>opstruss.lock</code> and what they mean; built-in tasks; the keywords and functions of expressions</td></tr><tr><td>Command line</td><td>Commands, built-in flags, exit codes, the JSON of <code>--json</code>: events and documents, and the <a href="./environment">variables</a> that it reads</td></tr><tr><td>Plugins</td><td>The <a href="./program-contract">program contract</a>: what a program gets and gives</td></tr><tr><td>State</td><td>Records, the <code>_meta</code> of each stack, <code>kv</code>, the file of each daemon, the shape of events, addresses, run ids, and the values of <code>outcome</code></td></tr><tr><td>Releases</td><td>The names of the files of a release, <code>checksums.txt</code>, and what each archive holds: <code>opstruss</code> at its root, with <code>LICENSE</code>, <code>README.md</code>, and <code>CHANGELOG.md</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="what-may-change" tabindex="-1">What may change <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-may-change" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What may change&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>What OpsTruss prints for people: the text at a terminal, colors, the layout of lines, and error messages. Scripts read <code>--json</code> instead.</li><li>Where files lie under <code>.opstruss/runs/</code>, <code>logs/</code>, and <code>work/</code>, and the cache in <code>~/.opstruss/</code>.</li><li>The API between the command line and the daemon, which both come from one binary.</li><li>What <code>OPSTRUSS_LOG=debug</code> prints.</li><li>What a version does not support yet, such as backend plugins and <code>mcp:</code>, which it refuses; a later version may support them.</li><li>A fix of a security problem, when nothing else fixes it.</li></ul><h2 id="how-opstruss-changes" tabindex="-1">How OpsTruss changes <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-opstruss-changes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How OpsTruss changes&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>It adds.</strong> A new key, field, variable, or command is optional, and without it OpsTruss behaves as before. Readers of events, plugins, and OpsTruss itself ignore fields and kinds of events they do not know. <code>outcome</code>, the <code>status</code> of a run, <code>level</code>, and <code>stream</code> keep their values, and details come in new fields; any other list of values may grow, as <a href="./exit-codes-and-json#json">Exit codes and JSON</a> says.</li><li><strong>Your names win.</strong> A built-in task, keyword, flag, or command that a later version adds gives way to a name of yours: a name in <code>uses:</code>, a task, or an input of the same name keeps working as before, and <code>validate</code> warns.</li><li><strong>It starts strict.</strong> Validation and limits start narrow and only grow looser, since what a version accepts, a later one accepts too.</li><li><strong>The level in <code>opstruss:</code>.</strong> Each file names the version of OpsTruss it is written for: <code>opstruss.yaml</code>, <code>opstruss.plugin.yaml</code>, and <code>opstruss.catalog.yaml</code>. An older OpsTruss refuses it before it reads the rest, so what a newer version added fails as the version it needs, and a newer one keeps the behavior of that level for a change that cannot be an addition. The <a href="https://github.com/opstruss/opstruss/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">changelog</a> names each such change.</li><li><strong>Data has versions.</strong> Everything OpsTruss writes has a version, which goes up only when an older OpsTruss would read the data wrong. An older OpsTruss refuses a newer version, and says to upgrade; a newer one reads every older version and migrates it when it writes. <code>opstruss.lock</code>, which a team shares, keeps its version unless what it holds needs a newer one.</li><li><strong>Schemas stay where they are.</strong> The schema of each minor version stays at <code>https://opstruss.github.io/v&lt;major&gt;.&lt;minor&gt;/schema/</code>; a patch release never makes a file that was valid there invalid. The first line that <code>opstruss init</code> writes in <code>opstruss.yaml</code> names <code>.opstruss/schema.json</code>, which each command that reads the project writes from the schema of its version and what the project uses, and stays at that path.</li><li><strong>Links stay.</strong> The page that the help of each command ends with, at <code>https://opstruss.github.io/v&lt;major&gt;.&lt;minor&gt;/reference/cli/&lt;command&gt;</code>, stays for as long as the site keeps that version; a page that moves leaves one behind that links to its new place.</li></ul><p>A change that breaks what this page promises comes only in a new major version, with its upgrade in the changelog under &quot;Upgrade notes&quot;.</p><h2 id="limits" tabindex="-1">Limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each limit only grows in a later version.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Limit</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Outputs of a program, from a file or a stream</td><td>1 MiB</td></tr><tr><td>An input in a variable of the environment; a larger one is only in the file of <code>OPSTRUSS_INPUTS</code></td><td>64 KiB</td></tr><tr><td><code>OPSTRUSS_RESULT</code></td><td>64 KiB</td></tr><tr><td>The output of programs that a run stores; the middle of more is dropped</td><td>10 MiB</td></tr><tr><td>A value of <code>kv</code>, and keys per stack</td><td>1 MiB, 1000</td></tr><tr><td>The body of a webhook request</td><td>1 MiB</td></tr><tr><td>An event of <code>--event</code></td><td>4 MiB</td></tr><tr><td>What <code>file()</code> reads</td><td>1 MiB</td></tr><tr><td>Runs that a daemon keeps waiting or running per task</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>Finished runs kept per task and stack, and the size of <code>runs/</code> per stack</td><td>100, 1 GiB</td></tr></tbody></table>',12)])])}const u=t(r,[["render",d]]);export{p as __pageData,u as default};
